,[7Cthe proof is complete. [0m[6B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[J[38;2;124;124;124mLet me analyze this problem carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe have $2^N$ countries ($N \geq 4$), each with a unique flag of $N$ fields [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(each yellow or blue). So flags are binary strings of length $N$, and there are [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$2^N$ of them (all possible).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA set of $N$ flags is "diverse" if they can be arranged into an $N \times N$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msquare (rows = flags) so that all $N$ fields on the main diagonal have the same [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcolor.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need to find the smallest $M$ such that among any $M$ distinct flags, there [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexist $N$ flags forming a diverse set.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. We have flags as binary vectors in $\{0,1\}^N$ (say [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m0=yellow, 1=blue). We select a subset $S$ of flags. We want to find $N$ flags in[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$S$ that can be arranged as rows of an $N \times N$ matrix such that the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdiagonal is monochromatic.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe diagonal being monochromatic means: there's a permutation $\pi$ of the $N$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mselected flags $f_1, \ldots, f_N$ such that $f_{\pi(i)}[i]$ is the same color [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfor all $i = 1, \ldots, N$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mEquivalently, we need $N$ flags and a permutation such that either all diagonal [0m[38;2;124;124;124mentries are 0 (yellow) or all are 1 (blue).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think in terms of bipartite matching. Given a set $S$ of flags, consider [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe bipartite graph where on one side we have flags in $S$ and on the other side[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwe have positions $1, \ldots, N$. We connect flag $f$ to position $i$ if $f[i] =[0m[38;2;124;124;124m0$ (for the yellow case). A diverse set with yellow diagonal corresponds to a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmatching of size $N$ in this bipartite graph (each position matched to a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdistinct flag with $f[i]=0$). Similarly for blue.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo we need: among any $M$ flags, either there's a matching of size $N$ in the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m"yellow" bipartite graph, or a matching of size $N$ in the "blue" bipartite [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgraph.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy König's theorem / Hall's theorem, a matching of size $N$ in the yellow graph [0m[38;2;124;124;124mfails iff there's a set of positions $T \subseteq [N]$ such that the number of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mflags in $S$ that have $f[i] = 0$ for some $i \in T$ is less than $|T|$. Wait, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlet me be more careful.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, we want a matching that covers all $N$ positions (each position gets a[0m[38;2;124;124;124mdistinct flag). By Hall's theorem, such a matching exists iff for every subset [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$T$ of positions, $|N(T)| \geq |T|$, where $N(T)$ is the set of flags in $S$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat have a 0 in at least one position of $T$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, actually Hall's theorem: we want to match positions to flags. Position $i$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcan be matched to flag $f$ if $f[i] = 0$. So the neighborhood of a set $T$ of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpositions is the set of flags $f \in S$ such that $f[i] = 0$ for some $i \in T$.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mHall's condition: for all $T \subseteq [N]$, $|N(T)| \geq |T|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$N(T) = \{f \in S : \exists i \in T, f[i] = 0\}$. The complement is $\{f \in S :[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\forall i \in T, f[i] = 1\}$, i.e., flags that are all-1 on positions $T$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $|N(T)| = |S| - |\{f \in S : f[i]=1 \forall i \in T\}|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHall's condition fails for yellow matching iff there exists $T$ with $|N(T)| < |[0m[38;2;124;124;124mT|$, i.e., $|S| - |\{f \in S : f|_T \equiv 1\}| < |T|$, i.e., $|\{f \in S : f|_T[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\equiv 1\}| > |S| - |T|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSimilarly, blue matching fails iff there exists $T'$ with $|\{f \in S : f|_{T'} [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\equiv 0\}| > |S| - |T'|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBoth fail iff:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- There exists $T$ with $|\{f \in S : f|_T \equiv 1\}| > |S| - |T|$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- There exists $T'$ with $|\{f \in S : f|_{T'} \equiv 0\}| > |S| - |T'|$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe want to find the maximum $|S|$ such that both can fail simultaneously, then [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$M = $ that max $+ 1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me denote $a(T) = |\{f \in S : f|_T \equiv 1\}|$ (number of flags that are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mall-1 on $T$) and $b(T') = |\{f \in S : f|_{T'} \equiv 0\}|$ (number of flags [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat are all-0 on $T'$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe want to maximize $|S|$ subject to: there exist $T, T'$ with $a(T) > |S| - |T|[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$ and $b(T') > |S| - |T'|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote $a(T) \leq 2^{N-|T|}$ (number of binary strings that are all-1 on $T$). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSimilarly $b(T') \leq 2^{N-|T'|}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAlso $a(T) \leq |S|$ and $b(T') \leq |S|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe conditions are:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$a(T) \geq |S| - |T| + 1$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$b(T') \geq |S| - |T'| + 1$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about what $S$ can be. The flags that are all-1 on $T$ form a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m"subcube" of dimension $N - |T|$. The flags that are all-0 on $T'$ form a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msubcube of dimension $N - |T'|$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor both conditions to hold, $S$ must contain many flags from the all-1-on-$T$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msubcube and many from the all-0-on-$T'$ subcube.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the overlap. A flag that is all-1 on $T$ and all-0 on $T'$: [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthis requires $T \cap T' = \emptyset$ (otherwise a position in both would need [0m
